the actual outer $x$ range. What is $a(x)\\times b(x)$?" :question/worked-solution "The set $S$ is retained exactly. A nonempty $y$ fiber requires $2\\le2xz$, so $xz\\ge1$. The inequality $0\\le z\\le2x$ then forces $x>0$. For a fixed admissible $x$, combine these requirements to obtain\n\n$$\\frac1x\\le z\\le2x.$$\n\nThis interval is nonempty precisely when $1/x\\le2x$, giving $1/\\sqrt2\\le x\\le2$. Every point in this base admits $2\\le y\\le2xz$. Thus the full nonempty $z$ fiber has endpoints $a(x)=1/x$ and $b(x)=2x$, with\n\n$$a(x)b(x)=\\frac1x(2x)=2.$$\n\nThe valid repeated integral is\n\n$$\\iiint_S f(x,y,z)\\,dV=\\int_{1/\\sqrt2}^{2}\\int_{1/x}^{2x}\\int_2^{2xz}f(x,y,z)\\,dy\\,dz\\,dx.$$\n\nFor $f=1$, integrating in $y$ and $z$ gives $4x^3-4x+1/x$, hence the volume is\n\n$$[x^4-2x^2+\\ln x]_{1/\\sqrt2}^{2}=\\frac{35}{4}+\\frac32\\ln2>0.$$\n\nThe authentic source solution copied the nominal pair $0,2x$ and reported its product $0$. Its formal repeated integral over $x\\in[-2,2]$ includes reversed and empty fibers; for $f=1$ it gives $0$, not the volume of $S$. The full nonempty fiber has endpoint product $2$."}]}
